# Owner-qualified repository paths the identity rules must not refuse.
#
# This repository is public and deliberately product-neutral, so the salted
# digests in commit-msg.forbidden-words refuse the first-party names anywhere in
# a commit message or a tracked file. The organisation that owns these
# repositories is named in their own URLs, and a URL cannot avoid naming its
# owner: a clone command, a badge target, a citation file, an action reference
# and a Go module path all have to spell it.
#
# So the permit is a PATH permit and not a word permit. The handle passes only
# where a slash and one of this family's repository names follow it. The handle
# standing alone is still refused, every other form of the name is still
# refused, and a sentence tying the organisation to anything is refused exactly
# as before, so a reader of a public repository sees an owner and learns nothing
# from it.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY DEFINITION, and that is the point of it. Three guards
# rule on the same strings -- .githooks/commit-msg (commit messages, as a hook
# and in CI), scripts/pre-push-identity-scan.py (pushed history) and
# scripts/forbidden-word-scan.py (tracked file contents). The two scanners held
# one hand-copied regex each and the hook held none, so the hook refused a
# module path the scanners explicitly permitted: a module-path rename could not
# state the path it was changing in the message of the commit that changed it.
# A permit is the one rule shape that can only ever loosen, and a loosening
# nobody notices looks exactly like the control working, so it is held in one
# place and asserted in both directions by scripts/pre-push-identity-scan-test.py.
#
# FORMAT: one permit per line, exactly two tab-separated fields,
# `<hex><TAB><regex>`. The compiled pattern is
# `bytes.fromhex(<hex>).decode("ascii") + <regex>`, case-insensitive. The owner
# half is held hex-encoded because all three readers, and this file, are
# themselves scanned by the guards above: a rule file that spells what its
# siblings forbid refuses its own commit. `#` comments and blank lines are
# ignored.
#
# An ABSENT file means no permitted paths at all. That is the fail-closed
# direction and it is what keeps the hook byte-shareable with a repository that
# has none. A MALFORMED line is a hard failure rather than a dropped permit: a
# guard whose rule surface failed to load has verified nothing, and a permit
# that failed to load reads as the guard working.
